  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "📸 Instagram Username Generator",
      "tagline": "Aesthetic, cute, cool and funny Instagram handles, names for themed accounts, and ideas from your own name."
    },
    "intro": "Your Instagram username is your @handle and the end of your profile link, so it does more work than the name above your bio. This **Instagram username generator** (an IG name generator, if you like) makes aesthetic, cute, cool and funny handles, names for art, food, travel, fitness and book accounts, and ideas from your own name, using only letters, numbers, full stops and underscores. It's an independent site and isn't connected to Instagram or its owner, Meta.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good Instagram usernames",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator, across eight of its styles:</p><ul><li><strong>slow.mornings</strong>: aesthetic, and it tells you what the grid will feel like.</li><li><strong>teeny.acorn</strong>: cute, short and easy to type.</li><li><strong>concrete.collective</strong>: cool and grown-up, for city photos or design.</li><li><strong>mostly.biscuits</strong>: a funny, honest bio in two words.</li><li><strong>the.gouache.journal</strong>: an art account that names the paint.</li><li><strong>sourdough.sunday</strong>: a food account with a weekly habit built in.</li><li><strong>coastal.footsteps</strong>: travel photos by the sea.</li><li><strong>margin.notes</strong>: a book account for people who write in their books.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "What an Instagram username can use",
        "content": "<p>Instagram usernames are made of letters, numbers, full stops and underscores, with no spaces. Instagram's own help pages suggest adding a full stop, a number or an underscore when the name you want is taken, which is why so many handles look like <em>sepia_postcards</em> or <em>the.quiet.archive</em>.</p><p>Two more things shape the names here:</p><ul><li><strong>No web addresses.</strong> Instagram's terms don't let you put a domain name or web address in your username without its permission. Lots of ordinary words are now web endings too (.art, .studio, .travel, .club), so no name here ends in one after a dot.</li><li><strong>Tidy dots.</strong> Every name starts and ends with a letter or number and never has two dots or underscores together. Every name is 30 characters or fewer.</li></ul><p>The <strong>name</strong> on your profile is separate from the username and can have spaces and capitals, so you can put your full name or a longer title there.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Aesthetic, cute, cool or funny?",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Aesthetic</strong> handles pair a soft, faded word with something you'd keep: <em>the.pressed.florals</em>, <em>sepia_postcards</em>, <em>the.quiet.archive</em>.</li><li><strong>Cute</strong> handles are tiny things with a small, round word in front: <em>teeny.acorn</em>, <em>little.pocket.macaron</em>.</li><li><strong>Cool</strong> handles sound like a design studio or a city quarter: <em>noir.society</em>, <em>the.brutalist.quarter</em>.</li><li><strong>Funny</strong> handles are honest about the account: <em>mostly.biscuits</em>, <em>biscuits.and.sarcasm</em>, <em>pigeon.appreciation.society</em>.</li></ul><p>The word <em>the</em> at the front makes almost any pair sound like a title, and it often frees up a handle that's taken without it.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Names for a themed account",
        "content": "<p>If your posts have one subject, put it in the handle. Choose <strong>Art</strong>, <strong>Food</strong>, <strong>Travel</strong>, <strong>Fitness</strong> or <strong>Books</strong> for names like <em>lino_works</em>, <em>fig.table</em>, <em>notes.from.the.harbour</em>, <em>morning.miles</em> and <em>the.bookish.edit</em>.</p><ul><li><strong>Name your craft, not your kit.</strong> <em>the.gouache.journal</em> says what you paint with; a paint brand's name in the handle would tie you to that company and could cause trouble with it.</li><li><strong>Leave room to grow.</strong> <em>fig.table</em> can cover any recipe; one dish in the handle might not.</li><li><strong>From your name</strong> works best for a portfolio or a small business: <em>sketches.by_Sam</em>, <em>Sam_Portfolio</em>, <em>the.Sam.Collective</em>.</li></ul><p>Naming a shop or studio rather than an account? The <a href=\"brand-name-generator.html\">Brand Name Generator</a> makes brandable words for products.</p>"
